  'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The Austin real estate market became one of the most talked-about in the country during its boom years, and the forces behind that run still shape what you will pay to buy or build there today. This updated guide skips the stale headline numbers and focuses on what drove the market, why it shifted, and what it means for buyers, owner-builders and investors now. For current prices, always pull fresh local data before you make a decision.</p>

<h2>What drove the Austin boom</h2>
<h3>Job growth and "Silicon Hills"</h3>
<p>Austin spent years attracting major tech employers, startups and the businesses that support them. Good-paying jobs pulled in people from other states and from across Texas, and every new hire needed somewhere to live. Growth also spread into healthcare, construction, education and services.</p>
<h3>In-migration and lifestyle appeal</h3>
<p>Live music, outdoor recreation, a strong food scene and no state personal income tax made Austin a magnet. Many newcomers came from higher-cost markets with home equity in hand, which helped push local prices up.</p>
<h3>Remote work</h3>
<p>When many companies moved to remote or hybrid work, people could choose where to live based on lifestyle instead of a commute. Austin and its suburbs were a popular landing spot.</p>
<h3>Low interest rates</h3>
<p>For a stretch, historically low mortgage rates stretched buyers' budgets. Combined with tight inventory, that fueled bidding wars and fast sales.</p>
<h3>Supply that could not keep up</h3>
<p>Land, labor, materials and permitting all take time. Builders worked hard to add homes, but demand outran supply for several years.</p>

<h2>Why the market shifted</h2>
<p>Booms do not last forever. As mortgage rates rose, many buyers' purchasing power dropped. At the same time, builders delivered a large wave of new homes and apartments, especially in the suburbs. The result was more inventory, longer days on market and more negotiating room in many areas. Some neighborhoods saw prices fall from their peaks while others held steadier.</p>
<p>The lesson is not that Austin is "good" or "bad." It is that local markets move in cycles, and the right decision for you depends on conditions when you buy and on how long you plan to stay.</p>
<div class="callout"><p><strong>Pro tip:</strong> Ignore national headlines about Austin. Ask your agent for three numbers in your target ZIP code: months of inventory, median days on market, and the average sale price as a percentage of list price. Those tell you far more about your negotiating power.</p></div>

<h2>What it means for buyers today</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Do your own comps.</strong> Look at recent closed sales near the specific homes you like, not citywide medians.</li>
<li><strong>Watch builder incentives.</strong> When new-home inventory builds up, builders may offer closing cost help or rate buydowns. Ask, and get it in writing.</li>
<li><strong>Count property taxes.</strong> Travis, Williamson and Hays counties all have their own rates, plus school and special districts. Taxes can shift your monthly payment noticeably.</li>
<li><strong>Think long term.</strong> Plan to stay several years so short-term swings matter less.</li>
<li><strong>Check flood risk.</strong> Central Texas is prone to flash flooding. Look at flood maps and get insurance quotes early.</li>
</ul>
<p>If you are new to buying, our <a href="/what-beginners-should-look-for-when-buying-a-house/">first-time buyer checklist</a> covers the basics, including how to estimate your full monthly cost.</p>

<h2>What it means for people building a home</h2>
<p>The boom changed the math for owner-builders too. Lot prices, labor and material costs all rose, and subcontractors got busy. In a cooler market, trades may have more availability, but land and materials do not always come back down.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Price the full build.</strong> Land, site work, utilities, permits, construction and a contingency. Our guide on <a href="/how-much-does-it-cost-to-build-a-house-in-texas/">how much it costs to build a house in Texas</a> breaks down the categories.</li>
<li><strong>Know the rules.</strong> City of Austin, suburban cities and unincorporated county land each have different permitting and code requirements. See <a href="/requirements-when-building-a-new-house-in-austin-texas/">requirements for building in Austin</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Compare build vs. buy.</strong> When builders are discounting finished homes, buying new inventory can beat a custom build on price. When resale inventory is tight, building may win.</li>
</ul>
<p>Good estimating is the best protection against a shifting market. A resource like [[amz:books_estimating|an estimating guide for residential building costs]] or [[amz:books_heldmann|Be Your Own House Contractor]] can help you build realistic numbers.</p>
<!--products:books_estimating,books_heldmann,home_planner|Resources for pricing and planning an Austin-area build-->

<h2>Lessons from the boom years</h2>
<p>Looking back, a few patterns stand out that are worth remembering in any hot market:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Waiving inspections was risky.</strong> Buyers who skipped inspections to win bidding wars sometimes inherited expensive problems. Protect yourself even when competition is fierce.</li>
<li><strong>Stretching for payments hurt later.</strong> Buyers who maxed out their budgets felt the squeeze when taxes, insurance and utilities rose.</li>
<li><strong>Location held up better.</strong> Homes close to jobs, good schools and amenities tended to be more resilient than those in far-flung areas with long commutes.</li>
<li><strong>Quality mattered.</strong> Rushed construction in a busy market showed up in punch lists and warranty claims. Careful inspections during the build paid off.</li>
</ul>

<h2>What it means for investors</h2>
<p>Austin's rental market saw a lot of new apartment supply, which can soften rents and increase concessions in some areas. Investors who bought at the peak with thin margins felt that. If you are investing, focus on cash flow at today's rents and rates, not on appreciation alone. Our post on <a href="/why-new-homes-are-a-great-starting-point-for-real-estate-investors/">why new homes are a strong starting point for investors</a> covers how to evaluate a deal.</p>

<h2>Where to find reliable data</h2>
<ul>
<li>The Austin Board of Realtors (Unlock MLS) publishes regular market reports.</li>
<li>The Texas Real Estate Research Center at Texas A&amp;M tracks housing data across the state.</li>
<li>County appraisal districts list property values and tax information for individual addresses.</li>
<li>A local agent can pull recent comparable sales for any neighborhood.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Next steps</h2>
<p>Pick the neighborhoods you care about, pull current data, and decide whether buying, building or renting a while longer fits your plans. If building is on your radar, start with our <a href="/budgeting-for-your-texas-home-build-tips-for-staying-on-track/">Texas home build budgeting guide</a> and the <a href="/free-training/">free training</a>.</p>
